\title{Phantom Topologies: Observer-Dependent Structure and the Two-Observer
Theorem for the Real Line}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-03}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We introduce \emph{phantom topologies}, a framework in which a single underlying
set carries a family of topologies indexed by a set of \emph{observers}, and the
``real'' (consensus) topology is defined as the structure on which all observers
agree. In the standard lattice of topologies --- ordered so that \emph{finer} means
\emph{smaller} --- the consensus is the supremum of the observer topologies, and a
set is consensus-open if and only if it is open for every observer. We prove three
facts. First, an \emph{agreement principle}: consensus-openness is exactly
unanimity of openness. Second, a \emph{coarsening principle}: every individual
observer is finer than the consensus, so enlarging the observer set can only
coarsen the agreed reality --- a counter-intuitive, order-reversing monotonicity.
Third, and centrally, a \emph{two-observer theorem}: the Euclidean topology on
$\R$ is exactly the consensus of two strictly sharper observers --- the lower-limit
(Sorgenfrey) observer, whose basic neighborhoods are right half-open intervals
$[x,b)$, and the upper-limit observer, whose basic neighborhoods are left half-open
intervals $(a,x]$. Neither observer alone recovers the Euclidean line, and the two
disagree, so the \emph{phantom number} of the real line --- the least number of
strictly sharper observers whose agreement rebuilds it --- is exactly two. We
develop the surrounding theory, give explicit counterexamples establishing the
lower bound, and state a program of conjectures.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The topology of a space is normally treated as an intrinsic, observer-independent
datum: a set of points $X$ is equipped with a fixed family of open subsets. This
paper explores a deliberate departure. We ask: \emph{what if the notion of
openness depended on who is observing the space?} We formalize this by attaching a
family of topologies to $X$, indexed by an abstract set of observers, and by
identifying the ``real'' topology with the consensus --- the topology on which
every observer agrees.

The motivating slogan is that \emph{reality is what all observers agree on}, while
each observer's private topology is what that individual ``sees.'' Our main
discovery is that this slogan is not merely evocative: the ordinary Euclidean real
line is \emph{literally} the consensus of two strictly sharper, mutually
incompatible observers, and no single observer suffices. Moreover, consensus is
order-reversing in resolution --- adding observers coarsens the agreed topology ---
giving a rigorous toy model of the idea that \emph{measurement coarsens
structure}, reminiscent of the collapse of a quantum superposition upon
observation.

\section{Preliminaries: the lattice of topologies}

Let $X$ be a set. A \emph{topology} $\tau$ on $X$ is a collection of subsets (the
\emph{open} sets) containing $\varnothing$ and $X$ and closed under arbitrary
unions and finite intersections. Write $\Top(X)$ for the set of all topologies on
$X$.

We use the convention, standard in lattice-theoretic treatments, that for $s, t
\in \Top(X)$ we write $t \le s$ to mean \emph{$t$ is finer than $s$} (every
$s$-open set is $t$-open). Under this order $\Top(X)$ is a complete lattice. The
key structural fact is the description of suprema: for a family $(T_i)_{i \in
\iota}$,
\begin{equation}
U \text{ is } \Big(\bigsqcup_i T_i\Big)\text{-open} \iff \forall i,\ U \text{ is }
T_i\text{-open}. \tag{$\star$}
\end{equation}

\section{Phantom topologies and consensus}

\begin{definition}[Phantom topology]
Let $\iota$ and $X$ be sets. A \emph{phantom topology} on $X$ with \emph{observer
set} $\iota$ is a function $T : \iota \to \Top(X)$, assigning to each observer $i$
a topology $T_i$ on $X$, its \emph{view} of $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Consensus topology]
The \emph{consensus} (or \emph{real}) topology of a phantom topology $T$ is
$\consensus(T) := \bigsqcup_{i \in \iota} T_i$, the supremum of the observer views.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Agreement principle]
For every phantom topology $T$ and every $U \subseteq X$,
\[
U \text{ is consensus-open} \iff \forall i \in \iota,\ U \text{ is }
T_i\text{-open}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the open-set description of suprema $(\star)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coarsening principle]
For every phantom topology $T$ and every observer $i$, $T_i \le \consensus(T)$;
i.e.\ each observer's view is finer than the consensus.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each element of a family lies below the supremum: $T_i \le \bigsqcup_j T_j$.
\end{proof}

The interpretation is counter-intuitive but exact: each observer resolves the
space at least as sharply as the agreed reality, and typically more sharply.
Enlarging the observer set can only \emph{coarsen} the consensus.

\begin{definition}[Phantom number]
Given a topological space $(X,\tau)$, its \emph{phantom number} is the least
cardinality of an observer set $\iota$ for which there exists a phantom topology
$T$ with each $T_i$ strictly finer than $\tau$ and $\consensus(T) = \tau$.
\end{definition}

\section{The two observers on the real line}

\begin{definition}[Lower-limit / Sorgenfrey observer]
A set $U \subseteq \R$ is \emph{lower-open} if
\[
\lowerOpen(U) :\iff \forall x \in U,\ \exists b,\ x < b \ \wedge\ [x,b) \subseteq U.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Upper-limit observer]
A set $U \subseteq \R$ is \emph{upper-open} if
\[
\upperOpen(U) :\iff \forall x \in U,\ \exists a,\ a < x \ \wedge\ (a,x] \subseteq U.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Both $\lowerOpen$ and $\upperOpen$ define topologies $\tau_L$ (the
\emph{lower-limit topology}) and $\tau_U$ (the \emph{upper-limit topology}) on
$\R$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
We verify the axioms for $\tau_L$; $\tau_U$ is symmetric. For the whole space, take
$b = x+1$. For finite intersections, if $[x, b_1) \subseteq S$ and $[x, b_2)
\subseteq T$ then $[x, \min(b_1,b_2)) \subseteq S \cap T$. For arbitrary unions, if
$[x,b) \subseteq U$ for a member $U$ then $[x,b)$ lies in the union. The dual
bookkeeping (with $\max(a_1,a_2)$) handles $\tau_U$.
\end{proof}

The lower-limit topology $\tau_L$ is the classical Sorgenfrey line; it is finer
than Euclidean, separable, first-countable, but not metrizable. The topology
$\tau_U$ is its reflection under $x \mapsto -x$.

\section{The two-observer theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Two-observer theorem]
Let $\tau_E$ denote the Euclidean topology on $\R$. Then $\tau_L \sqcup \tau_U =
\tau_E$; i.e.\ a set $U \subseteq \R$ is Euclidean-open if and only if it is both
lower-open and upper-open. Equivalently, the two-element phantom topology $T$ with
$T_L = \tau_L$, $T_U = \tau_U$ satisfies $\consensus(T) = \tau_E$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the agreement principle, $U$ is $(\tau_L \sqcup \tau_U)$-open iff $U$ is both
lower- and upper-open.

\textbf{($\Rightarrow$) The squeeze.} Suppose $U$ is both lower- and upper-open and
fix $x \in U$. Lower-openness gives $b > x$ with $[x,b) \subseteq U$;
upper-openness gives $a < x$ with $(a,x] \subseteq U$. Put $\varepsilon :=
\min(x-a,\ b-x) > 0$. If $|y-x| < \varepsilon$ and $y \ge x$, then $y < x +
\varepsilon \le b$, so $y \in [x,b) \subseteq U$; if $y < x$, then $y > x -
\varepsilon \ge a$, so $y \in (a,x] \subseteq U$. Hence $(x-\varepsilon,
x+\varepsilon) \subseteq U$ and $U$ is Euclidean-open.

\textbf{($\Leftarrow$)} If $U$ is Euclidean-open and $x \in U$, choose
$\varepsilon>0$ with $(x-\varepsilon, x+\varepsilon) \subseteq U$. Then $b =
x+\varepsilon$ witnesses lower-openness and $a = x-\varepsilon$ witnesses
upper-openness.
\end{proof}

The heart of the argument is the identity $(a,x] \cup [x,b) = (a,b)$, which turns
two one-sided commitments into a single two-sided neighborhood.

\section{Sharpness: the phantom number of $\R$ is exactly two}

\begin{proposition}[No single observer suffices]
$[0,1)$ is lower-open but not Euclidean-open, so $\tau_L \ne \tau_E$; and $(0,1]$
is upper-open but not Euclidean-open, so $\tau_U \ne \tau_E$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$[0,1)$ is lower-open (take $b=1$). At $0$, every ball $(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)$
meets the complement, so $[0,1)$ is not Euclidean-open. The other case is
symmetric at the point $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The observers disagree]
$\tau_L \ne \tau_U$: the set $[0,1)$ is lower-open but not upper-open.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
At $x=0$ there is no $a<0$ with $(a,0] \subseteq [0,1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The phantom number of $(\R, \tau_E)$ is exactly $2$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both $\tau_L, \tau_U$ are strictly finer than $\tau_E$ (finer by the
($\Leftarrow$) direction of the two-observer theorem, strict by the previous
proposition), and their consensus is $\tau_E$, so two suffice. A single strictly
finer observer $T_0$ has $\consensus = T_0 \ne \tau_E$, so one does not.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion: measurement coarsens structure}

The coarsening principle gives the framework a physical flavor. Each observer's
private topology is richer than the consensus: distinctions such as the one-sided
openness of $[0,1)$ vanish when observers agree. Forming a consensus is a
\emph{collapse} of fine, observer-dependent structure to a coarser shared
topology, mirroring quantum measurement. The real line emerges as a maximally
symmetric consensus, since $x \mapsto -x$ exchanges $\tau_L$ and $\tau_U$.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Phantom number as a cardinal invariant.} Conjecture: every
metrizable space without isolated points has phantom number two; discrete spaces
zero; the Sierpi\'nski space one.
\item \textbf{A Galois connection behind consensus.} Conjecture: the
family-to-consensus map is one half of a Galois connection whose fixed points
classify achievable consensus topologies; the order-reversing monotonicity is the
signature of an adjunction.
\item \textbf{Dense linear orders.} Conjecture: on every densely ordered set
without endpoints, the agreement of the two half-open topologies is the order
topology; density is necessary, completeness is not.
\item \textbf{The cofinite (Zariski-like) plane needs a third observer.}
Conjecture: the cofinite topology on an infinite set cannot be recovered from two
strictly sharper observers; its phantom number is at least three.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Phantom topologies formalize the intuition that the structure of a space can depend
on the observer, with the ``real'' space recovered as unanimous agreement. The
Euclidean real line is exactly the consensus of two strictly sharper, mutually
contradictory observers, and its phantom number is exactly two. Consensus is
order-reversing in resolution, so agreement coarsens structure --- a rigorous echo
of measurement collapse.

